WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.733
SPEAKER: Autoscaling
helps you size up

00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:03.930
to handle increased
demand and size down

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.538
to avoid paying for resources
that you're not using.

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:08.080
That's not just
limited to your pods.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.470
It can also apply
to the machine side.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.660
Today, we'll talk about
autoscaling infrastructure

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.540
with Google Kubernetes Engine
and cover some best practices.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.210
Let's go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.030
In the last video,
we covered how

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.570
autoscaling works and went over
horizontal and vertical pod

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.710
autoscaling.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.210
If you haven't seen
it, I definitely

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.770
recommend checking
it out, since we'll

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.895
look at some additional
ways that you can configure

00:00:36.895 --> 00:00:38.850
autoscaling and how
that can help you

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.530
with cost optimization.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.440
I'll start by going over the
cluster autoscaler and then

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.320
something really cool,
node autoprovisioning.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.850
Then we'll wrap
up with some tips

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.650
to use when setting
up your autoscaling.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.580
So if pod autoscalers can
help you resize or add

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.280
additional pods, what can you
do with the infrastructure side?

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.370
Well, just like the
pod autoscalers,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.070
you can think of the same
horizontal and vertical options

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.690
for infrastructure.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.840
This time, we're talking about
changing the number of nodes

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.210
or automatically
creating additional node

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.560
pools with optimized size.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.010
This is really where the
cloud and Kubernetes work well

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.200
together, since the
cloud tends to give you

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.790
a lot more flexibility
with your resources.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.210
Let's start with horizontally
adding more nodes, which is

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.720
done by the cluster autoscaler.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.810
The cluster autoscaler is
designed to add or remove nodes

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.290
based on demand.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.230
But this time,
demand is actually

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.930
based on pod scheduling
and requests.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.570
If additional pods aren't able
to be put into the existing

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.910
nodes that are running,
the cluster autoscaler

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:41.600
will create a new node, so
that the pods can get placed.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.040
You can and should use the
cluster autoscaler along

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.860
with the horizontal or
vertical pod autoscalers.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.010
That way, as you create
more or larger pods,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.530
your infrastructure can
grow alongside them.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.260
It's also important to note
that the cluster autoscaler is

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.990
perfect for cost optimization
since it is actually

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.862
built to prefer lower costs.

00:02:00.862 --> 00:02:02.320
That means that
the autoscaler will

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.120
start by trying to
create additional nodes

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.700
of the cheapest type and then
move to more expensive node

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.755
types if it can't
create the cheaper ones.

00:02:08.755 --> 00:02:10.630
Isn't it great when cost
optimization is just

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.680
built right in?

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.500
Just like the pod autoscaler,
having an appropriate pod

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.350
disruption budget is important
for having an effective cluster

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.700
autoscaler.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.250
The budget defines the
minimum number of running pods

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.280
for your workload.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.117
So when the cluster
autoscaler is removing nodes,

00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:27.950
you can ensure that
your workload will still

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.670
be running correctly.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.210
Now, there are some pods
that can't be restarted,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.100
which will then block
the cluster autoscaler

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.630
from removing a node.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.290
System pods, like Kube-DNS and
pods that use local storage,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.350
are examples of pods that
can't be restarted by default.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.410
For system pods, you can set
a pod disruption budget, which

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.720
will allow them to be
rescheduled on another node,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.390
freeing up their original
node to be deleted.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.240
But you may not want to set
a budget for some system pods

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.160
that only have one replica,
like metric server,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.240
since restarting them can
cause temporary disruptions.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.510
For pods with local storage, you
can enable a special annotation

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:06.110
called safe to evict to let
the cluster autoscaler know

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.768
that they're safe
to be restarted.

00:03:07.768 --> 00:03:10.310
If you have any pods that are
supposed to run for a long time

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.740
without being restarted, you
might want to move those over

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.570
to a separate node
pool, so they don't

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.170
block the cluster autoscaler.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.390
There's also a few more
things to think about when

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.580
using the cluster autoscaler.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.170
Make sure to figure
out how to read

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.810
the cluster autoscaler logs,
so that you can understand

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.910
any unexpected actions.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:29.530
If your workload
can support it, see

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.840
if you can use preemptible
VMs, since those

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.060
will be prioritized by
the cluster autoscaler

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.430
and could save you
a lot of costs.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.087
But don't forget to have
a backup node pool in case

00:03:39.087 --> 00:03:41.170
there's any trouble with
creating new preemptibles

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.490
when they're needed.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.560
Set your minimum
nodes by figuring out

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.320
the minimum capacity needed
to keep your workloads running

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.460
without disruption, so you
don't end up with idle nodes.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.340
So the cluster autoscaler
helps add and remove nodes

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.770
in response to pods
needing to be rescheduled.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.130
However, GKE specifically
has another feature to scale

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.430
vertically called
node autoprovisioning.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.460
Node autoprovisioning
actually adds new node pools

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.560
that are sized to meet demand.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.930
Without node autoprovisioning,
the cluster autoscaler

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.720
will only be able to create
new nodes in the node pools

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.520
that you've specified.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.769
This is perfect for
helping optimize resource

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:19.600
usage for workloads
and other apps

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.570
that don't need extreme
scaling since creating

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.330
an optimized node pool
might take some more time

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:29.060
than just adding additional
nodes to an existing pool.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.760
It's pretty cool to have node
pools created based on what

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.500
best fits your application.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.180
And as long as your apps
support interruptions,

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.540
you can save even more
costs by setting your pods

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.540
to be preemptible tolerant,
allowing the auto provisioner

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.500
to create preemptible
node pools.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.350
A few other things
to keep in mind.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.150
Consider how each
of your autoscalers

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.010
will affect your
apps, and make sure

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.140
that you spent the time
to configure each of them

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.400
properly.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.170
New node pools can
take a bit longer

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.870
to be created than simply
adding more pods or nodes,

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:59.540
so you may want to
adjust the metric value

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.710
of your horizontal
pod autoscaler

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.620
to create additional
pods sooner.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.780
Set minimum and
maximum resource sizes

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.050
based on what your apps need, so
that the node auto provisioner

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.150
doesn't interfere with
keeping your workload running.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.950
With all four types
of autoscalers

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.770
that we've talked about, there's
a lot of configuration options

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.920
that you can use to
customize your GKE clusters.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.050
Effective use of
some or all of these

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.020
can really maximize
your cost optimization.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.120
But it's also
important to understand

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.440
how long it might take
to scale resources,

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.753
especially if you're
expecting large spikes.

00:05:32.753 --> 00:05:34.420
Let's walk through
what scaling up looks

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.310
like when demand increases.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.040
For both horizontal and
vertical pod autoscaling,

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.280
they'll respond by adding pods
or resizing pods, depending

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.740
on the settings that we talked
about in the last video.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.510
On the infrastructure
side, if there's

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.670
room on your existing nodes that
have already deployed your app,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.640
it might be able to
skip pulling the image

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.673
and just start running
the application.

00:05:53.673 --> 00:05:55.090
If you're working
with a node that

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.970
hasn't deployed your application
before, a bit of time

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.280
might be added if it needs
to download the container

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.480
images before running it.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.140
But if you don't have enough
room on your existing nodes

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.120
and you're using
cluster autoscaler,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:08.960
it could take even longer.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.840
Now it needs to
provision a new node,

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.230
set it up, and then download
the image and start up pods.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.900
If the node auto provisioner
is going to create a new node

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:18.910
pool, there will
be even more time

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.260
as you provision the
new node pool first

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.740
and then go through all the
same steps for the new node.

00:06:26.447 --> 00:06:28.280
In order to handle these
different latencies

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.900
for autoscaling,
you'll probably want

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:31.490
to overprovision
a little bit, so

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.550
that there's less pressure
on your apps when scaling up.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.110
This is really important
for cost optimization

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.110
because you don't want
to pay for more resources

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.070
than you need.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.758
But you also don't want
your apps to struggle.

00:06:42.758 --> 00:06:45.050
Let's go through two strategies
for figuring out how to

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.073
over provision using a
pretty simple formula.

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:49.490
We'll start with
a formula you can

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.520
use to find an optimized
target for using horizontal pod

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.463
autoscaling.

00:06:53.463 --> 00:06:55.880
The first thing we'll want is
an appropriate buffer target

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:57.755
for the application to
ensure that everything

00:06:57.755 --> 00:06:59.010
is running smoothly.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.460
Let's choose a buffer
size of about 15%,

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:03.650
so that we can keep the
application safely away

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.992
from 100% CPU utilization.

00:07:05.992 --> 00:07:08.450
Then we can take a look at the
percentage of traffic growth

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.880
that we might expect in a
two- to three-minute period.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.210
Here, let's say that we
expect a growth of about 30%.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:15.750
With these numbers
in the formula,

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.410
we can calculate a target for
the horizontal pod autoscaler

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.510
around 65%.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.820
This generally means
that we'll be about 35%

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.220
over provisioned in order
to appropriately handle

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.770
the expected traffic growth
while still minimizing

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.420
extra resources.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.210
If you're using larger
nodes and smaller workloads

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.860
with consistently less
traffic than requested,

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.380
you could consider
over provisioning

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.360
a bit less since
there might be room

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.300
to go above 100% utilization.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:44.120
The second strategy
is to use pause pods.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.728
These are low-priority
deployments,

00:07:45.728 --> 00:07:48.020
which mean that they could
be removed and then replaced

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:50.000
by high-priority deployments.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.800
Since these pause pods aren't
actually doing anything,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.080
they're basically
just reserving buffer.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.050
When the higher
priority pod needs room,

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:59.240
the pause pod will be
removed and rescheduled

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.250
to another node or a new node.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.020
And the higher priority
pod has the room

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.150
it needs to be
scheduled quickly.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.520
Using these can help
because node spin up time

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.490
can happen without affecting
the high-priority pods.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.320
It's basically like
scaling a little bit

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:14.210
ahead of when you
need to since they'll

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.310
be spun up before
they're needed, thanks

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.050
to those low-priority pots.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:21.530
It's a good idea to try and
only use one pause pod per node,

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.840
so it's quick to be
rescheduled, the API

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.940
isn't overloaded with
even more requests,

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.180
and the full node will
be available quickly.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.700
All right, that's a lot to
think about for autoscaling.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:34.100
And "pause pod" is
really hard to say.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.039
Once again, it's important to
know what your apps can handle

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:38.940
and to optimize around that.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.745
But you can also
optimize your apps.

00:08:40.745 --> 00:08:42.620
On the next video, we'll
cover different ways

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:43.668
to do exactly that.

00:08:43.668 --> 00:08:45.710
Remember to check out the
link in the description

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:49.750
for the full guide on
GKE cost optimization.

